<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">

<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#FFFFFF" />
    <base href="/" />
    @* No static <title> here: every page supplies its own through <PageTitle>, and with
       prerendering the HeadOutlet emits a real <title> element into this head. A second,
       earlier one would win in the served HTML (browsers and crawlers take the first). *@
    <meta name="description" content="bit Belemetry - one lightweight, OpenTelemetry-native telemetry SDK for every Blazor app: page views, web vitals, errors, fetch spans, ILogger, Activity and Meter, exported over OTLP or to Azure Monitor, with per-feature GDPR consent." />

    @* What a link to the site looks like when it is shared. *@
    <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
    <meta property="og:site_name" content="Bit.Belemetry" />
    <meta property="og:title" content="Belemetry - telemetry for every Blazor app" />
    <meta property="og:description" content="Browser and .NET telemetry for Blazor, OpenTelemetry-native without the OpenTelemetry packages: OTLP, Azure Monitor, per-feature consent." />
    <meta property="og:url" content="https://belemetry.bitplatform.dev" />
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary" />

    <ResourcePreloader />
    @* The site kit (app.css, theme.js, site.js - shared by every Utilities demo), then this demo's
       own sample styles. Bit.Belemetry itself ships no stylesheet. *@
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["app.css"]" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["demo.css"]" />
    @* Scoped-CSS bundle: includes the client project's *.razor.css files as well. *@
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["Bit.Belemetry.Demo.Server.styles.css"]" />
    @* Synchronous on purpose: applies the light/dark theme before first paint. *@
    <script src="@Assets["theme.js"]"></script>
    @* Before any module script: an import map added after one has started loading is ignored. *@
    <ImportMap />

    @* Belemetry's browser script, as early as possible: it starts before Blazor boots, so it sees the
       initial page load, the web vitals and any early error. It writes its configuration (built from
       the options registered in Program.cs) into the page and loads the module. The module is given
       its fingerprinted address - the one the import map above hands <BelemetryInitializer /> when it
       imports the same module once the app is interactive - so the two are one module, started once.
       <BelemetryInitializer /> in Routes.razor connects it to .NET. *@
    <BelemetryScript Src="@Assets["_content/Bit.Belemetry/bit-belemetry.js"]" />

    <link rel="icon" href="favicon.ico" type="image/x-icon" />
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" sizes="512x512" href="icon-512.png" />
    @* Same render mode as the app itself, so <PageTitle> keeps working after hydration. *@
    <HeadOutlet @rendermode="InteractiveWebAssembly" />
</head>

@* data-enhance-nav="false": a link followed before the WebAssembly runtime has started is a plain
   page load rather than an enhanced one. Enhanced navigation fetches and merges the next page while
   the runtime is still starting, and the router the runtime then attaches can render the address
   it read before the merge - the URL says /web-app and the page is the landing page. Once the
   app is interactive its router handles every link itself and this attribute is not consulted. *@
<body data-enhance-nav="false">
    @* Prerendered on the server, then hydrated by the WebAssembly runtime. The .NET half of Belemetry
       runs in the browser from then on; the prerender pass only renders. *@
    <Routes @rendermode="InteractiveWebAssembly" />

    <div id="blazor-error-ui" data-nosnippet>
        An unhandled error has occurred.
        <a href="." class="reload">Reload</a>
        <span class="dismiss">&#128473;</span>
    </div>

    <script src="@Assets["_framework/blazor.web.js"]"></script>
    <script src="@Assets["site.js"]"></script>
    @* The one helper the instrumentation pages need beyond the kit: the triggers they press. *@
    <script src="@Assets["demo.js"]"></script>
</body>

</html>
